Concrete/README.md:60:**`compile` is defined as the composition of the stage functions listed in `Stages.lean`**, so a pass that isn't registered can't be in the pipeline. `Output` is pure data with `DecidableEq`: `{ toks, ast, p, sites, tokens, mir, mem, bir, ll, lcb }`. There are no proofs inside it, and every theorem is stated *about* `compile`.
Concrete/README.md:64:**CheckedCore is the single admission point.** Trap admission, termination and fact derivation run there, and nowhere else. The checker also emits one **discharged-evidence token** per operation it proved safe; in slice 0 that is `known (v)` for a `+` whose value is statically known. Tokens are carried through MIR, Memory CFG and BIR. A token is validated (`token_valid`). Its soundness connects the checker's known value to the run-time operands through `knownExpr_sound`, because `knownExpr_u8` alone establishes only static knowledge. Only a validated token lets a pass omit a check. No pass removes a check without evidence (PASS-9).
Concrete/README.md:66:**Place MIR** is where later slices put evaluation order, cleanup edges and the expansion of residual checks. In slice 0, an add with no valid token becomes an explicit `add.ovf` plus a branch to a trap site, and an add with a valid token becomes a plain add. Every accepted slice-0 program has tokens on all its adds, so its generated IR (MIR through BIR and both backend inputs) contains no trap path. Whether the executable contains trap code is an artifact fact, not established yet. Unchecked well-scoped programs, such as `200 + 56` in gate 6, have no tokens, so the check-and-trap path is still proved and tested.
Concrete/README.md:70:**Typed source identity, end to end.** The lexer and parser mint typed `SiteId`s and spans. Elaboration records an origin edge for every generated temporary in `out.sites`. Every MIR, Memory CFG and BIR instruction carries its origin, and so does every trap site. Diagnostics, facts, JSON and certificates all read this one evidence graph. Core itself has no positions yet (spec open items O1/O4), so the site map sits beside `out.p` without changing the spec's Core.
Concrete/README.md:77:| Parser | one direct theorem for the generic table-driven LL(1) parser |
Concrete/README.md:85:**One Core, no slice-0 syntax.** The spec has one Core syntax; a slice is two decidable predicates, `Fragment.surface` (the grammar productions it supports) and `Fragment.core` (the Core constructors it supports) (A2i plan, item I3). Elaboration produces the spec's Core directly. Admission checks `Fragment.core s0 p` together with the spec's `check`, and the slice theorems (`slice0_exits`, `slice0_never_traps`) are stated over `Fragment.core s0 p ∧ Checked p`. The "not supported in slice 0" refusal comes from `Fragment.surface s0`. The compiler keeps no Core syntax of its own and no `toCore`.
Concrete/README.md:94:- **trap realization**: the kind plus the typed site (an encoded result word; see the runtime below);
Concrete/README.md:101:- a `trap k` terminator.
Concrete/README.md:103:Every BIR construct has a direct LLVM spelling (`@llvm.uadd.with.overflow.i8`) and a direct LCB-IR expansion: a widened add, a compare, and a branch to the trap block. No construct exists only for one backend.
Concrete/README.md:115:2. *Instructions*: the invariant is: `env.size = f.valueWidths.size = valueCount p`, every width is `word`, and for every register `r` with BIR `lookup benv r = some v` (the newest binding: BIR's list shadows on loops where LCB overwrites a slot), `env[id r] = some (enc v)`, and every current integer binding `.int t x` is in range (`t.inRange x`: `Agrees` alone would let a `.u8` register hold `2^32`, whose word is 0, and then the overflow flags differ; immediates are in range by WF, results by `wrap_inRange`, and block arguments carry it). From the instruction's first position, the expansion's steps reach the next position with the invariant extended by the result (and flag); every operand read succeeds at width `word`, and BIR's operands stay in range (for `flag_word`). Needs: register ids injective, intermediate ids disjoint from register ids, every id below `valueCount`. Proved so far: the environment lemmas (`Agrees`, `read_operand`, `write_ok`, `agrees_set_other`, `agrees_define`) and the step lemmas for each expansion, from its first position with word-width slots: `bin_steps` (4 steps, `word (wrap t (x op y))` at `n + 3`), `binOvf_steps` (6 steps, also `enc (.bool (!t.inRange (x op y)))` at `n + 5`, for in-range operands) and `trunc_steps` (3 steps, `word (wrap to x)` at `n + 2`). With the numbering facts (`numberingOk`: intermediates are no register's id, every id is below `valueCount`), `inst_sim` carries the invariant (agreement, range, size) through any one instruction, with the result (and flag) bound, in `Inst.width ins` LCB steps (the adapter's `Inst.width`). Layer 3 so far: the adapted body's shape (`adapt_block`, `adapt_widths`, `block_insts`: instruction `i`'s expansion starts at `instPos b i`; `block_size`: the terminator follows the last one) and `body_sim`: the LCB steps of a block's instructions keep the invariant through BIR's `execInsts`. Terminators: `ret_sim` and `trap_sim` (LCB returns `enc v`, and the word `256 + s`), and `transfer_ok`: along an edge whose every phi has one arm from the predecessor (WF's `t ≠ e`), LCB reads every phi from the old environment and then writes them. `edge_sim`: along the only edge `j → t` (WF's `t ≠ e` makes a taken `condbr` arm the only one), LCB's `transfer` lands at `t`'s first position with the invariant for BIR's `enter`: each parameter's slot holds its new value, every other register keeps its slot. `runBlock_sim` composes them over BIR's `runBlock` (by fuel): an exit or a trap is matched by LCB returning its encoding, running out of fuel by at least `fuel` LCB steps (each block transition is at least one LCB step: the terminator's `transfer`). Layer 4: `initial_ok` (the entry block, no phis since WF's entry has no parameters, an empty environment of size `valueCount p`) and **`adapter_correct_run`**: for `WF p` and `adapt p = .ok f`, `run p fuel = exited c` gives `HasBehavior f #[] (.returned (word c))`, `trapped s` gives `.returned (word (256 + s))`, and running out of fuel at every fuel gives `.diverges` (`steps_prefix`). All under LCB's own `Source` semantics, with only `propext`, `Classical.choice` and `Quot.sound`. Then, closing the statement:
Concrete/README.md:116:   - *BIR progress* (`Concrete/BIR/Progress.lean`): `run_progress`: for `WF p`, every `run p fuel` is `outOfFuel` or `Outcome.Encodable` (an exit below 256, a trap at a site of a table of at most `maxSites`), so never `stuck`; the invariant `Typed` (every register in the block's scope is bound to a value of its type, in range) is block-local, extended by each instruction as `Block.wf` extends the scope and established at entry from the arguments (registers distinct, from WF's `eraseDups` check). `behaviour_diverges_iff` (any program): `behaviour p = .diverges` iff `run p fuel = .outOfFuel` at every fuel.
Concrete/README.md:118:   - *Bridge*: `adapter_correct_behaviour`: the adapted body returns the word `w` with `behaviour p = decode p.sites w` (by `decode_encode`, which keeps the site lookup and the trap kind), or diverges exactly when BIR diverges.
Concrete/README.md:122:3. *Blocks*: `execInsts` against those steps; `ret` and `trap` against LCB's `returned`; `br`/`condbr` against `transfer`: the target exists, each phi has exactly one arm from this predecessor (WF's `t ≠ e`), destinations are distinct (distinct registers), and all arms are read from the OLD environment before any destination is written, which is BIR's `enter` (all arguments evaluated before binding), including loop-carried swaps; the new state is at position 0.
Concrete/README.md:123:4. *Runs*: under `WF p` and `adapt p = .ok f`, `Source.initial f #[] = .ok s₀` (entry block exists with no phis, no parameters, an empty environment of size `valueCount p`); then `run p fuel = exited c` or `trapped s` gives `HasBehavior f #[] (.returned (word (encode o)))`. Divergence: each BIR block transition is at least one LCB `Step` (BIR fuel counts blocks, LCB counts instructions and terminators), so BIR running out of fuel at every fuel gives arbitrarily long LCB prefixes, i.e. `HasBehavior f #[] .diverges`. BIR progress (a WF program never gets stuck) is needed only to make exit, trap and divergence exhaustive.
Concrete/README.md:127:LCB's own lowering, assembler and linker are LCB's named trust boundaries, carried as `P_toolchain[lcb]` until LCB's preservation theorem covers the slice-0 domain.
Concrete/README.md:131:**The encoded result, with one decoding runtime.** Neither backend's input contains a call or a trap.
Concrete/README.md:135:- a trap at site `s` returns `256 + s.idx`. The runtime's site table maps the index to the trap's kind, typed `SiteId` and span.
Concrete/README.md:137:This fits LCB's verified source profile (`ssa-int-v2`, `LeanCompilerBackend/Verified/Source.lean`), which has integer operations, compares, branches, phis, a return or divergence, and no calls, traps or memory. It is also what lets the adapter be proved against LCB's semantics as it stands.
Concrete/README.md:139:It also separates `return 134` from a trap: they return the words 134 and 256.
Concrete/README.md:143:- otherwise, it writes the trap line for `k` to fd 2 and exits 134.
