docs/decisions.md:1254:**Only the project owner decides these items, OD1 – OD157.** All are answered. OD1 – OD32 (exit 2n, closed) and OD47 were decided on 2026-10-29 (OD23 – OD32 by the strictness pass of that day). OD33 – OD157 were decided on 2026-10-31, by the owner's rule that an answer every reviewer agreed on and as strict as Austral or stricter is taken, and on 2026-11-01, when the owner approved the final set for the rest; the authority decision of the same day supersedes OD38's and OD156's `Root` readings (OD94 was withdrawn: MOD-1 already refuses its case, bug 337). The answers are recorded in [docs/decisions.md](decisions.md), state the decided rules in chapters now (owner revision, 2026-11-03), then become Lean in phases B–D, and are enforced as each new-compiler slice lands. The questions raised while recording them were answered on 2026-11-02, the owner deciding the first four and, by the owner's rule of that day, Claude and Codex agreeing on the best long-term answer for the rest ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02)); they revise OD24, OD38, OD43, OD83, OD102, OD146 and OD156 and the authority rules. The spec audit's questions were answered the same day ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-audit)), revising OD140 and STMT-13. Traits are exported and imported like any other declaration (owner, 2026-11-02). Core was provisionally approved on 2026-11-05 ([record](#decided-2026-11-05-core)); A2b formalization is current, and the A2c freeze awaits the four design theorems, the A2i integration stage (2026-11-06) and an outside-reader pass. New questions follow the same rule: No agent, orchestrator or
docs/decisions.md:1300:| OD10 | <a id="od10"></a>Arithmetic: checked by default, named wrapping and saturating operations, shifts with a checked count; an abort runs no deferred call | T-ARITH, recorded as settled (today's behaviour), with §4.5's abort rule | exits 2h and 2j, so V1a, V1b and V1c (checked `+ - *` and their traps); R-0493 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: integer arithmetic is checked by default; wrapping and saturating arithmetic are named operations; the shift count is checked; an abort runs no deferred call **Revised 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): plain arithmetic is admitted only with evidence that it cannot fail; wrapping, saturating and checked (`Result`) forms cover the rest; overflow is still never silent.** |
docs/decisions.md:1337:| OD47 | <a id="od47"></a>Loops: `while` and a counted range loop (added 2026-10-29) | the current compiler has `while` and the C-style `for (init; cond; step)`, `for (cond)` and `for { }`; bug 331 | exit 2q; R-0499; V1a's loops and exits | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: (1) the C-style `for` is removed in every form; (2) loops are `while cond { ... }` and `for i from a until b { ... }`: half-open (`a`, ..., `b - 1`, empty when `a >= b`), the index immutable in the body, `a` and `b` evaluated once before the first iteration, the index of the bounds' integer type (both bounds of one type), `break` and `continue` as usual (`continue` advances to the next index), `from` and `until` reserved (whether reserved or contextual was reopened by the reviews of that day: OD50), LL(1) as `for` IDENT `from` expr `until` expr block with the restricted head expression for `b` (OD16); (3) in Core the counted loop desugars to the one `while` form, carrying its termination measure `b - i`. Now STMT-6@3, STMT-11@2 and STMT-12 (pending, bug 331) |
docs/decisions.md:1339:| OD49 | <a id="od49"></a>The arithmetic of a counted loop's termination measure (added 2026-10-29, while recording OD47). Options: (a) the measure is a mathematical integer in the proof only, never computed at run time; (b) it is computed in the unsigned type of the bounds' width; (c) a counted loop whose `b - a` cannot be represented is refused. Recommendation: (a): the loop compares `i < b` and increments `i`, which cannot overflow below `b`, so the measure need not exist at run time Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): (a) a mathematical integer in the proof only, never computed at run time | the counted loop is not in the current compiler | bug 331's repair; the contracts chapter's loop variant | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Mathematical integer in the proof only |
docs/decisions.md:1358:| OD68 | <a id="od68"></a>Failure inside a deferred call (added 2026-10-30, item review new 5 of the owner picks of that day). Question: What OWN-12's text says about a failure inside a deferred call, given that an abort runs no deferred call (STMT-1, STMT-2). Options: (a) OWN-12 states that a trap inside a deferred call ends the program and runs no further deferred call, as STMT-2 and OD10 say for every abort; (b) the remaining deferred calls still run after a trapping one, which would amend STMT-2 and OD10. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): none given (raised by the review). Austral: not checked | failure is abort only (STMT-2); OWN-12's rationale leaves the case open | OWN-12, STMT-1, STMT-2, STMT-11 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Trap ends the program, no further deferred call |
docs/decisions.md:1370:| OD80 | <a id="od80"></a>`assume` in the grammar (added 2026-10-30, item C3 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether the grammar gains the proof-annotation statement `assume expr;`. Options: (a) add it to the grammar; (b) remove it from the parser, so the language loses the form; (c) keep the recorded divergence for now. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): individual review required; no pick recorded. Austral: not checked | the parser accepts it | CON-2, CON-3, SYN-1 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Keep `assume`, tracked: every claim depending on it is marked assumed **Qualified 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): `assume` cannot admit an operation in safe code.** |
docs/decisions.md:1372:| OD82 | <a id="od82"></a>Loop contract attributes and where attributes attach (added 2026-10-30, item C5 (R7) of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether the grammar gains `#[invariant(e)]` and `#[variant(e)]` before a loop, and whether attributes attach by kind (declaration attributes to the next declaration, loop attributes to the next loop). Options: (a) add them to the grammar and attach attributes by kind; (b) remove them from the parser; (c) keep the recorded divergence for now. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (a): attach by kind, declaration attributes to the next declaration, `#[invariant]` and `#[variant]` to the next loop. Austral: not checked | the parser accepts them | CON-2, STMT-12, SYN-1 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Add; attributes attach by kind **Extended 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): `#[variant]` also attaches to a function, one measure per recursive cycle; `#[may_not_terminate("reason")]` also attaches as a prefix to an expression (an indirect call), through one attribute-prefix production in statements.** |
docs/decisions.md:1382:| OD92 | <a id="od92"></a>A capability set inferred from callbacks (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing CALLABLE_VALUES and HANDLE_CAPABILITIES). Question: Whether a combinator may write its capability set as that of its callbacks (`with(f)`), the checker computing the union of the sets of the callbacks its body calls. Bears on TYPE-D1, TYPE-17, TRUST-4. The current compiler: no such form; `with(f)` reads `f` as a capability name (an unresolved one, which MOD-1 refuses and the current compiler accepts: bug 337), so a combinator names a capability parameter (`fn map<T, U, cap C>(.., f: fn(&T) with(C) -> U) with(C)`). The callable-values design (2026-06-12, [CALLABLE_VALUES_AND_CAPABILITIES.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/CALLABLE_VALUES_AND_CAPABILITIES.md) §6) recorded the shortcut as specified and not built, after [research/language/capability-polymorphism.md](../legacy/research/language/capability-polymorphism.md); no roadmap item tracks it. Options: (a) no shortcut, combinators name capability parameters; (b) add it, as set propagation and not row unification. Austral: not applicable (capabilities are values). Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-92 | see OPEN-92 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): No shortcut; name a capability parameter |
docs/decisions.md:1383:| OD93 | <a id="od93"></a>Widening a function value's capability set (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing CALLABLE_VALUES and HANDLE_CAPABILITIES). Question: Whether a function value converts to a function type whose capability set contains its own, implicitly by subset or by an explicit conversion. Bears on TYPE-4, TYPE-17, TRUST-4. The current compiler: refused (E0220): `fn(Int) -> Int` is not a `fn(Int) with(Console) -> Int`, nor `with(File)` a `with(Std)`, and there is no explicit conversion; a capability-polymorphic parameter accepts either by instantiating its variable. The callable-values design (§10) says there is no implicit widening, and that "widening is explicit or via lattice subsumption only", with a lattice example (`Concurrent ⊇ Async`) whose names are not capabilities; HANDLE_CAPABILITIES R7 keeps handle types invariant, as TYPE-4 does. Options: (a) exact types, as in the current compiler; (b) subsumption into a larger set; (c) an explicit conversion. Austral: not applicable (capabilities are values). Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-93 | see OPEN-93 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Exact types **Canonical current capability-set rule: authority rule 4; this earlier exact-types answer refers to it.** |
docs/decisions.md:1384:| OD94 | <a id="od94"></a>Names in `with(...)` that are not capabilities (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing CALLABLE_VALUES and HANDLE_CAPABILITIES). Question: Whether a name in `with(...)` that is not a capability, a capability alias or a capability variable in scope is refused. Bears on TRUST-D1, TYPE-D1, TRUST-4. The current compiler: accepted as a capability of its own: `fn main() with(Foo) -> Int` compiles and runs, and a call to a function that requires `Foo` is refused only in a caller that does not list it (E0520). The compiler's `validCaps`, TRUST-4's rationale and [SAFETY.md](../legacy/docs/verification/SAFETY.md) name nine capabilities. Options: (a) refuse an unknown name; (b) an open vocabulary. Austral: not applicable (capabilities are values). Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of 2026-10-30). | [MOD-1](../legacy/docs/spec/modules.md#MOD-1) refuses the name; the current compiler accepts it (bug 337) | MOD-1@1 | withdrawn 2026-10-30: not an owner question; MOD-1@1 refuses the name (bug 337) |
docs/decisions.md:1385:| OD95 | <a id="od95"></a>What `Time`, `Env`, `Process` and `Alloc` mean (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing CALLABLE_VALUES and HANDLE_CAPABILITIES). Question: What authority each of `Time`, `Env`, `Process` and `Alloc` names. Bears on TRUST-D1, TRUST-4. The current compiler: no document states them; HANDLE_CAPABILITIES §2 defined `Console`, `File` and `Network`, and SAFETY.md `Random` (TRUST-D1). std requires `Time` for its clock and sleep functions, `Env` for environment variables and for `std.net`'s `bind` and `connect`, `Process` for exit, spawn, wait and kill, and `Alloc` for heap allocation. Options: state each as std uses it, or narrow or split it (std's `Env` on socket functions is the case to settle). Austral: not applicable (capabilities are values). Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-95 | see OPEN-95 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): State each; move socket `bind`/`connect` from `Env` to `Network` |
docs/decisions.md:1386:| OD96 | <a id="od96"></a>Two foreign bindings of one C symbol (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing CALLABLE_VALUES and HANDLE_CAPABILITIES). Question: Whether two foreign bindings of one C symbol (`#[link_name]`) must agree on its C signature. Bears on FFI-3, FFI-5. The current compiler: accepted: `abs_a(x: i32) with() -> i32` and `abs_b(x: i64) with() -> i64`, both bound to `abs`, compile and the binary runs, while the interpreter stops at run time on the mismatched width. HANDLE_CAPABILITIES §5 (encoding A, one binding per effect for one C symbol) planned to refuse a mismatch with a diagnostic. Options: (a) refuse; (b) allow, each binding's signature trusted on its own. Austral: not applicable. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-96 | see OPEN-96 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Refuse |
docs/decisions.md:1387:| OD97 | <a id="od97"></a>A build profile that omits or demands an overflow check (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing ARITHMETIC_POLICY and VALUE_MODEL). Question: Whether a build profile may omit the run-time check of an integer operation that no proof discharges, or refuse such an operation at compile time. Bears on ARITH-3, ARITH-9. The current compiler: every profile emits every check, and no option turns one off; ARITH-3's rationale says a profile changes reports, never code generation, and OD10 decided that arithmetic is checked "by default". The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §3.1 instead let a release profile omit a check that is "explicitly configured off and reported", and a provable or high-integrity profile refuse an unproved overflow site. Options: (a) every profile keeps every check that no discharged proof removes; (b) a release profile may turn checks off when configured and reported, never wrapping; (c) as (a), and a profile may also refuse an operation whose no-overflow obligation is not discharged. Austral: not checked. Raised by the absorption of ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md (2026-10-30). | see OPEN-97 | see OPEN-97 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Keep every check; a profile may refuse undischarged ops **Subsumed 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): every build refuses an operation without discharged evidence.** |
docs/decisions.md:1391:| OD101 | <a id="od101"></a>What a trap writes (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing ARITHMETIC_POLICY and VALUE_MODEL). Question: Whether a run-time trap writes a message naming the operation, its operands and its source location before the process ends, and in what form. Bears on STMT-2, STMT-7, ARITH-3. The current compiler: the binary writes nothing and ends by `SIGABRT`, and `--interp` reports a run-time error naming the trap (`arithmetic overflow (checked +)`). The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §9.2 described a message `concrete: arithmetic overflow in function 'f' at file:line` with the operation, the operands and the policy. Options: (a) the binary writes nothing; (b) it writes one line naming the trap and its location; (c) the §9.2 form. Austral: not checked. Raised by the absorption of ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md (2026-10-30). | see OPEN-101 | see OPEN-101 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): One line naming the trap and location. Its form, decided 2026-11-06: [runtime row](#decided-project-runtime-2026-11-06) |
docs/decisions.md:1397:| OD107 | <a id="od107"></a>What a float-to-integer rounding gives for NaN, infinities and out-of-range values (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether each float-to-integer rounding operation (`trunc`, `round`, `floor`, `ceil`) gives a value or an error for NaN, an infinity or a result outside the target type's range, and which value. Bears on ARITH-1, ARITH-11. The current compiler: the rounding operations do not exist (R-0500); a float-to-integer `as` truncates toward zero and aborts on NaN, an infinity or a value out of range, natively and in `--interp`. The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §8.2 required a stated result for every input, "either as a value or as an error". Options: (a) an error, as `try_convert` reports; (b) a value: the nearest value of the type for an out-of-range input or an infinity, and 0 for NaN; (c) a trap, as `as` does today. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | the rounding operations do not exist; a float-to-integer `as` truncates toward zero and aborts on NaN, an infinity or a value out of range | ARITH-1, ARITH-11 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): An error, as `try_convert` |
docs/decisions.md:1398:| OD108 | <a id="od108"></a>A capability listed twice in a set (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether a capability set that lists one capability twice (`with(Console, Console)`) is the same set as the one that lists it once, is refused, or is a different set. Bears on TYPE-D1, TYPE-17, TYPE-4. The current compiler: accepted where it is written, and a different type: `fn(Int) with(Console) -> Int` is not a `fn(Int) with(Console, Console) -> Int`, in either direction (E0220), because `CapSet.normalize` sorts a set and does not remove a repeated name. The callable-values design ([CALLABLE_VALUES_AND_CAPABILITIES.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/CALLABLE_VALUES_AND_CAPABILITIES.md)) calls the capabilities of a function type a set. Options: (a) the same set; (b) refuse a repeated capability where it is written; (c) a different set, as in the current compiler. Austral: not applicable (capabilities are values). Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | accepted, and a different set: `with(Console, Console)` is not `with(Console)` (E0220) | TYPE-D1, TYPE-17, TYPE-4 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Refuse a repeated capability |
docs/decisions.md:1399:| OD109 | <a id="od109"></a>Specializations that differ only in a function type's capabilities (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether the symbol of a generic function's instantiation includes the capability set of a function-type argument, so that two instantiations that differ only in that set have distinct symbols. Bears on TYPE-17, TYPE-18. The current compiler: one: `pick<T: Copy>` instantiated at `fn(Int) -> Int` and at `fn(Int) with(Console) -> Int` emits the single function `pick_for_Fn_T_Int_to_Int_E`, because Mono's `tyToSuffix` drops a function type's capability set while it keeps a struct's capability arguments (E0809's history), and the program runs correctly since capabilities have no run-time representation (TYPE-18). The archived [HANDLE_CAPABILITIES.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/HANDLE_CAPABILITIES.md) R8 required collision-free symbol identity across instantiations and left code sharing to its own argument. Options: (a) the set is part of the symbol, as for struct capability arguments; (b) it is not, one symbol serving both, stated as erasure with its correctness argument. Whether two symbols share one body stays an implementation choice (TYPE-18). Austral: not applicable (capabilities are values). Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | one specialization: Mono's symbol drops a function type's capability set | TYPE-17, TYPE-18 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Set is part of the symbol |
docs/decisions.md:1400:| OD110 | <a id="od110"></a>Enums at the foreign boundary (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether a value of enum type may cross an `extern fn` boundary, and if so in what representation. Bears on FFI-3, FFI-7. The current compiler: refused: `extern fn f(x: E)` with `enum Copy E { A, B }` is E0574 (a non-FFI-safe parameter); only scalars, raw pointers and `repr(C)` structs cross. The design record [LAYOUT_CONTRACT.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/LAYOUT_CONTRACT.md) says enums must not cross (§3.2); before it was archived it also said they cross as integers. Options: (a) refused, as in the current compiler; (b) a fieldless enum crosses as an integer of a stated width, which needs a stated discriminant for each variant. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | refused (E0574) | FFI-3, FFI-7 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refused |
docs/decisions.md:1406:| OD116 | <a id="od116"></a>A checked total function category (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the verification documents). Question: Whether the language has a `total fn` category, a runtime function checked to terminate without trapping that contracts may then call. Bears on CON-1, CON-3, CON-5. The current compiler: there is no such category (`total fn f(..)` is a parse error, E0001); a contract clause may still call a capability-free runtime function until CON-3's judgment lands. [CONTRACTS_AND_VCS.md](../legacy/docs/verification/CONTRACTS_AND_VCS.md) (Design Principles) distinguishes `spec fn`, a checked `total fn`, ordinary and trusted functions, and admits a `total fn` into contracts once its obligations close. Options: (a) add the category; (b) no category: contracts call only `spec fn` (CON-3's full judgment). Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/verification, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-116 | see OPEN-116 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): No category |
docs/decisions.md:1407:| OD117 | <a id="od117"></a>`old`, quantifiers and implication in contracts (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the verification documents). Question: Whether contract expressions gain `old(x)`, quantifiers and an implication or equivalence operator, and with what syntax. Bears on CON-2, CON-3, SYN-1. The current compiler: `#[ensures(result == old(x))]` compiles and runs, and `--report contracts` marks the clause `invalid_contract_expression` (unknown function `old`); `forall i: Int, ...` is a parse error (E0001); there is no implication operator. [CONTRACTS_AND_VCS.md](../legacy/docs/verification/CONTRACTS_AND_VCS.md) (Contract Expression V1) lists all three as deferred. Options: (a) add each, with a syntax decided per form; (b) keep them out, a claim needing one being a Lean theorem; (c) refuse `old(..)` now as an unknown name, deciding the rest later. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/verification, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-117 | see OPEN-117 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): No `old`, quantifiers or implication in contracts; state such properties as Lean theorems |
docs/decisions.md:1408:| OD118 | <a id="od118"></a>A proven run-time violation in trusted code (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the verification documents). Question: Whether an index the compiler proves out of bounds, or a division it proves to be by zero, is refused in `trusted` and `with(Unsafe)` code as in safe code. Bears on MEM-6, ARITH-3, TRUST-5, TRUST-12. The current compiler: `a[5]` on a `[Int; 3]`, or `x / 0`, is refused in safe code (E0900, `error[runtime-safety]`) and compiles in a `trusted fn` (the index also in a function declaring `Unsafe`), where it aborts at run time on both consumers. MEM-6 states the refusal with no exemption; [KNOWN_HOLES.md](../legacy/docs/verification/KNOWN_HOLES.md) C7 records the exemption as intended. Options: (a) refuse everywhere, as MEM-6 reads; (b) keep the exemption and state it in MEM-6. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/verification, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-118 | see OPEN-118 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refuse everywhere **Subsumed 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): refusal of proven violations becomes refusal of unproven safety, everywhere.** |
docs/decisions.md:1409:| OD119 | <a id="od119"></a>Buffered output when the process aborts (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the verification documents). Question: Whether output the program wrote before a trap or abort is flushed before the process ends. Bears on STMT-1, STMT-2, PASS-13, OPEN-101. The current compiler: print builtins write through C's buffered stdio, and `abort` does not flush it on glibc (a binary that prints `hello` and then traps prints nothing on Linux) while it does on Darwin; the interpreter models the same buffer and the same platform difference. STMT-2 says a failure ends the process at once and is silent on output already written, so the observable trace of a trapping program differs by platform. Recorded by [INTERPRETER_TRUST.md](../legacy/docs/verification/INTERPRETER_TRUST.md) §2. Options: (a) everything written before the trap is output (flush on abort, or unbuffered output); (b) what was written is unspecified on a trap, and PASS-13 compares output only for exiting runs. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/verification, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-119 | see OPEN-119 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Everything written before the trap is output |
docs/decisions.md:1410:| OD120 | <a id="od120"></a>A vacuous contract (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the verification documents). Question: Whether a contract whose precondition is unsatisfiable is refused by the language or only reported, under a project policy. Bears on CON-1, CON-2. The current compiler: `#[requires(x > 5 && x < 3)]` compiles; a decided-unsatisfiable precondition makes the claim `vacuous`, never `proved`, and E0613 refuses it only under the `forbid-vacuous` policy (`Concrete.Policy`), not in a plain build. [CONTRACTS_AND_VCS.md](../legacy/docs/verification/CONTRACTS_AND_VCS.md) (Soundness Bridge) states the reporting rule. Options: (a) a language refusal in every build; (b) tooling only: always reported `vacuous`, refused under a policy, as in the current compiler. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/verification, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-120 | see OPEN-120 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Tooling: reported `vacuous`, refused under policy **Qualified 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): a vacuous precondition used for safety admission is refused in every build.** |
docs/decisions.md:1412:| OD122 | <a id="od122"></a>A `repr(C)` struct returned from an extern function (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the platform and stdlib documents). Question: Whether a `#[repr(C)]` struct returned from an `extern fn` is returned as the platform's C calling convention returns it, at every size. Bears on FFI-2, FFI-11. The current compiler: a returned struct of at most 8 bytes comes back as one 64-bit integer, stored as 8 bytes into the struct's slot even when the struct is smaller (a 4-byte `in_addr` returned by `inet_makeaddr` reads back wrong natively); a larger one is returned as an LLVM aggregate, which matches C on x86-64 and AArch64 only for some layouts. [ABI.md](../legacy/docs/platform/ABI.md) promised returns of at most 8 bytes and listed larger ones as not promised. Options: (a) every size, as C, classified by the `Layout` authority (R-0502); (b) at most 8 bytes (or 16), a larger return refused in an `extern fn` signature; (c) none, a returned struct going through an out-pointer the binding declares. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/platform and docs/stdlib, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-122 | see OPEN-122 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Every size per C ABI via `Layout` |
docs/decisions.md:1415:| OD125 | <a id="od125"></a>Contracts at run time (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the platform and stdlib documents). Question: Whether a `#[requires]`, `#[ensures]` or loop `#[invariant]` that no proof discharges is checked at run time, trapping when it fails, or is only a claim. Bears on CON-1, CON-2, STMT-2. The current compiler: contracts are erased metadata: a call that violates `#[requires(x > 0)]` runs normally, in the binary and in `--interp`, and `assert` elaborates to proof evidence only (syntax chapter introduction). [ERROR_CONVENTIONS.md](../legacy/docs/stdlib/ERROR_CONVENTIONS.md) lists contract-violation traps among the fatal failures. Options: (a) claims only, never checked at run time, as in the current compiler; (b) checked at run time and trapping unless discharged; (c) checked under a build profile only (OPEN-97, OPEN-124). Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/platform and docs/stdlib, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-125 | see OPEN-125 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Claims only **Qualified 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): a claim used for safety admission needs closed evidence.** |
docs/decisions.md:1416:| OD126 | <a id="od126"></a>Target assumptions (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the platform and stdlib documents). Question: Which target facts are language rules: 64-bit pointers, little-endian byte order, POSIX hosts, and the sizes of `bool`, `Unit` and pointers. Bears on FFI-2, FFI-7, TYPE-19, TYPE-20. The current compiler: one target profile: `Layout` hardcodes 8-byte pointers and references, `bool` of 1 byte, `Unit` of 0 bytes, an `i32` enum tag, and assumes little-endian POSIX hosts; FFI-2 names AAPCS64 for the native profile, and the integer types' widths follow from their names (TYPE-19). [EXECUTION_MODEL.md](../legacy/docs/platform/EXECUTION_MODEL.md) and [ABI.md](../legacy/docs/platform/ABI.md) state these as platform facts. Options: (a) language rules, a 32-bit or big-endian target being another language profile; (b) target facts, observable only through `sizeof`, `alignof` (FFI-7) and named target constants; (c) as (b), with the sizes of `bool` and `Unit` fixed by the language. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/platform and docs/stdlib, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-126 | see OPEN-126 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Language rules; other targets are other profiles. Generalized 2026-11-08 by the [foundation decision](#foundation-q3): profiles are read-only and separate address width, pointer representation, length width, byte order and address spaces; the existing 64-bit little-endian profile and its `i32` enum tags are unchanged |
docs/decisions.md:1422:| OD132 | <a id="od132"></a>Polymorphic recursion (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the compiler documents and the grammar comments). Question: Whether a generic function that calls itself at a strictly larger type argument (`f::<Box<T>>` inside `f<T>`) is refused, and by which check. Bears on TYPE-13, PASS-7 and PASS-12. The current compiler: such a program is not refused; compilation does not terminate (more than 200 s and 2 GB on 2026-10-30, natively and with `--interp`), since monomorphization specializes without a bound on function instances (the bound `closeInstances` has covers only generic type definitions). [PASSES.md](../legacy/docs/compiler/PASSES.md) §7 lists unbounded polymorphic recursion as Mono's error. Options: (a) refuse a call cycle whose type arguments grow, before Mono; (b) refuse at Mono when specialization exceeds a stated limit. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/compiler, 2026-10-30; the non-termination may also be filed as a bug). | see OPEN-132 | see OPEN-132 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Refuse growing call cycles before Mono |
docs/decisions.md:1437:| OD147 | <a id="od147"></a>`repr(C, packed)` together (added 2026-10-30, raised by the spec quality audit of 2026-10-30). Question: Whether `#[repr(C, packed)]` is accepted, and what layout it gives. Bears on FFI-4 and FFI-8. The current compiler: accepted: `#[repr(C, packed)] struct Copy P { a: u8, b: u32 }` has size 5, so packing overrides C alignment. FFI-4 refuses only `packed` with `align(N)`, and FFI-8 states C layout and packed layout separately. Options: (a) refuse the combination; (b) accept it as C field order with no padding and alignment 1, as the current compiler does, and FFI-8 says so. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the spec quality audit, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-147 | see OPEN-147 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Accept: C order, no padding, align 1, stated in FFI-8 |
