1337:| OD47 | <a id="od47"></a>Loops: `while` and a counted range loop (added 2026-10-29) | the current compiler has `while` and the C-style `for (init; cond; step)`, `for (cond)` and `for { }`; bug 331 | exit 2q; R-0499; V1a's loops and exits | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: (1) the C-style `for` is removed in every form; (2) loops are `while cond { ... }` and `for i from a until b { ... }`: half-open (`a`, ..., `b - 1`, empty when `a >= b`), the index immutable in the body, `a` and `b` evaluated once before the first iteration, the index of the bounds' integer type (both bounds of one type), `break` and `continue` as usual (`continue` advances to the next index), `from` and `until` reserved (whether reserved or contextual was reopened by the reviews of that day: OD50), LL(1) as `for` IDENT `from` expr `until` expr block with the restricted head expression for `b` (OD16); (3) in Core the counted loop desugars to the one `while` form, carrying its termination measure `b - i`. Now STMT-6@3, STMT-11@2 and STMT-12 (pending, bug 331) |
1339:| OD49 | <a id="od49"></a>The arithmetic of a counted loop's termination measure (added 2026-10-29, while recording OD47). Options: (a) the measure is a mathematical integer in the proof only, never computed at run time; (b) it is computed in the unsigned type of the bounds' width; (c) a counted loop whose `b - a` cannot be represented is refused. Recommendation: (a): the loop compares `i < b` and increments `i`, which cannot overflow below `b`, so the measure need not exist at run time Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): (a) a mathematical integer in the proof only, never computed at run time | the counted loop is not in the current compiler | bug 331's repair; the contracts chapter's loop variant | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Mathematical integer in the proof only |
1372:| OD82 | <a id="od82"></a>Loop contract attributes and where attributes attach (added 2026-10-30, item C5 (R7) of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether the grammar gains `#[invariant(e)]` and `#[variant(e)]` before a loop, and whether attributes attach by kind (declaration attributes to the next declaration, loop attributes to the next loop). Options: (a) add them to the grammar and attach attributes by kind; (b) remove them from the parser; (c) keep the recorded divergence for now. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (a): attach by kind, declaration attributes to the next declaration, `#[invariant]` and `#[variant]` to the next loop. Austral: not checked | the parser accepts them | CON-2, STMT-12, SYN-1 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Add; attributes attach by kind **Extended 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): `#[variant]` also attaches to a function, one measure per recursive cycle; `#[may_not_terminate("reason")]` also attaches as a prefix to an expression (an indirect call), through one attribute-prefix production in statements.** |
1382:| OD92 | <a id="od92"></a>A capability set inferred from callbacks (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing CALLABLE_VALUES and HANDLE_CAPABILITIES). Question: Whether a combinator may write its capability set as that of its callbacks (`with(f)`), the checker computing the union of the sets of the callbacks its body calls. Bears on TYPE-D1, TYPE-17, TRUST-4. The current compiler: no such form; `with(f)` reads `f` as a capability name (an unresolved one, which MOD-1 refuses and the current compiler accepts: bug 337), so a combinator names a capability parameter (`fn map<T, U, cap C>(.., f: fn(&T) with(C) -> U) with(C)`). The callable-values design (2026-06-12, [CALLABLE_VALUES_AND_CAPABILITIES.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/CALLABLE_VALUES_AND_CAPABILITIES.md) §6) recorded the shortcut as specified and not built, after [research/language/capability-polymorphism.md](../legacy/research/language/capability-polymorphism.md); no roadmap item tracks it. Options: (a) no shortcut, combinators name capability parameters; (b) add it, as set propagation and not row unification. Austral: not applicable (capabilities are values). Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-92 | see OPEN-92 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): No shortcut; name a capability parameter |
1383:| OD93 | <a id="od93"></a>Widening a function value's capability set (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing CALLABLE_VALUES and HANDLE_CAPABILITIES). Question: Whether a function value converts to a function type whose capability set contains its own, implicitly by subset or by an explicit conversion. Bears on TYPE-4, TYPE-17, TRUST-4. The current compiler: refused (E0220): `fn(Int) -> Int` is not a `fn(Int) with(Console) -> Int`, nor `with(File)` a `with(Std)`, and there is no explicit conversion; a capability-polymorphic parameter accepts either by instantiating its variable. The callable-values design (§10) says there is no implicit widening, and that "widening is explicit or via lattice subsumption only", with a lattice example (`Concurrent ⊇ Async`) whose names are not capabilities; HANDLE_CAPABILITIES R7 keeps handle types invariant, as TYPE-4 does. Options: (a) exact types, as in the current compiler; (b) subsumption into a larger set; (c) an explicit conversion. Austral: not applicable (capabilities are values). Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-93 | see OPEN-93 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Exact types **Canonical current capability-set rule: authority rule 4; this earlier exact-types answer refers to it.** |
1384:| OD94 | <a id="od94"></a>Names in `with(...)` that are not capabilities (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing CALLABLE_VALUES and HANDLE_CAPABILITIES). Question: Whether a name in `with(...)` that is not a capability, a capability alias or a capability variable in scope is refused. Bears on TRUST-D1, TYPE-D1, TRUST-4. The current compiler: accepted as a capability of its own: `fn main() with(Foo) -> Int` compiles and runs, and a call to a function that requires `Foo` is refused only in a caller that does not list it (E0520). The compiler's `validCaps`, TRUST-4's rationale and [SAFETY.md](../legacy/docs/verification/SAFETY.md) name nine capabilities. Options: (a) refuse an unknown name; (b) an open vocabulary. Austral: not applicable (capabilities are values). Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of 2026-10-30). | [MOD-1](../legacy/docs/spec/modules.md#MOD-1) refuses the name; the current compiler accepts it (bug 337) | MOD-1@1 | withdrawn 2026-10-30: not an owner question; MOD-1@1 refuses the name (bug 337) |
1385:| OD95 | <a id="od95"></a>What `Time`, `Env`, `Process` and `Alloc` mean (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing CALLABLE_VALUES and HANDLE_CAPABILITIES). Question: What authority each of `Time`, `Env`, `Process` and `Alloc` names. Bears on TRUST-D1, TRUST-4. The current compiler: no document states them; HANDLE_CAPABILITIES §2 defined `Console`, `File` and `Network`, and SAFETY.md `Random` (TRUST-D1). std requires `Time` for its clock and sleep functions, `Env` for environment variables and for `std.net`'s `bind` and `connect`, `Process` for exit, spawn, wait and kill, and `Alloc` for heap allocation. Options: state each as std uses it, or narrow or split it (std's `Env` on socket functions is the case to settle). Austral: not applicable (capabilities are values). Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-95 | see OPEN-95 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): State each; move socket `bind`/`connect` from `Env` to `Network` |
1386:| OD96 | <a id="od96"></a>Two foreign bindings of one C symbol (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing CALLABLE_VALUES and HANDLE_CAPABILITIES). Question: Whether two foreign bindings of one C symbol (`#[link_name]`) must agree on its C signature. Bears on FFI-3, FFI-5. The current compiler: accepted: `abs_a(x: i32) with() -> i32` and `abs_b(x: i64) with() -> i64`, both bound to `abs`, compile and the binary runs, while the interpreter stops at run time on the mismatched width. HANDLE_CAPABILITIES §5 (encoding A, one binding per effect for one C symbol) planned to refuse a mismatch with a diagnostic. Options: (a) refuse; (b) allow, each binding's signature trusted on its own. Austral: not applicable. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-96 | see OPEN-96 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Refuse |
1398:| OD108 | <a id="od108"></a>A capability listed twice in a set (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether a capability set that lists one capability twice (`with(Console, Console)`) is the same set as the one that lists it once, is refused, or is a different set. Bears on TYPE-D1, TYPE-17, TYPE-4. The current compiler: accepted where it is written, and a different type: `fn(Int) with(Console) -> Int` is not a `fn(Int) with(Console, Console) -> Int`, in either direction (E0220), because `CapSet.normalize` sorts a set and does not remove a repeated name. The callable-values design ([CALLABLE_VALUES_AND_CAPABILITIES.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/CALLABLE_VALUES_AND_CAPABILITIES.md)) calls the capabilities of a function type a set. Options: (a) the same set; (b) refuse a repeated capability where it is written; (c) a different set, as in the current compiler. Austral: not applicable (capabilities are values). Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | accepted, and a different set: `with(Console, Console)` is not `with(Console)` (E0220) | TYPE-D1, TYPE-17, TYPE-4 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Refuse a repeated capability |
