branches/D-trusted-base.diff:344:-9. **Trusted base** (owner, 2026-11-07): a golden test that `concrete facts` prints exactly the spec's premise list, so a premise added, removed or reworded anywhere fails until the golden is updated with it. Implemented provisionally in `scripts/checks/compiler/slice0_trusted_base.sh`: until `concrete facts` exists, `scripts/checks/compiler/slice0_trusted_base.lean` prints `Premises.report`: the premise registry `Concrete/Premises.lean`, the sorted axioms of each headline theorem (`Premises.theorems`) and each axiom's type; the gate appends the Lean toolchain and the Lake pins, and the result must equal `tests/golden/slice0_trusted_base.txt` byte for byte. The README's open-premise table must equal the registry's rendering (`Premises.tableRow`). Controls cover the comparison, a failing printer, the axiom collection and a drifted table. The statements are text until `FrontendPremises` and `NativePremises` land as Lean structures; each entry then names its field.
branches/D-trusted-base.diff:345:+9. **Trusted base** (owner, 2026-11-07): `Concrete/Premises.lean` is the one list of trusted components and open premises. Until `concrete facts` exists, `scripts/checks/compiler/slice0_trusted_base.lean` prints `Premises.report`: components, premises, the sorted axioms of each headline theorem (`Premises.theorems`) and each axiom's type; the gate appends the Lean toolchain and the Lake pins, and the result must equal `tests/golden/slice0_trusted_base.txt` byte for byte. The README's component and open-premise tables must equal their registry renderings. Gate 9 also audits every `Concrete` declaration's axiom closure and every `extern`, `implemented_by`, `opaque`, `unsafe` or `partial` declaration against the component list. On Linux it traces every manifest `bir:` emission and native build and refuses an exec'd program the list does not name; other hosts print that tools were not checked. Controls cover golden drift, a failing printer, an extra axiom, an unlisted extern, an unlisted tool, a removed clang entry and drifted tables. **Every slice brief must list the trusted components it adds or removes; gate 9 fails until `Concrete/Premises.lean` names them.** The premise statements are text until `FrontendPremises` and `NativePremises` land as Lean structures; each entry then names its field.
branches/D-trusted-base.diff:346: 10. **Sanitizers** (owner, 2026-11-07; UBSan dropped by the owner, 2026-11-08; implemented in `scripts/checks/compiler/slice0_artifacts.sh`): every LLVM example also runs built with ASan in CI, and must give the same observation with no sanitizer report. ASan instruments only functions marked `sanitize_address`, which clang's C front end adds and the printed IR lacks, so the sanitizer build adds it to every definition (`slice0_native.sh sanitize`); a control body with an out-of-bounds load must be reported. There is no UBSan leg: UBSan's checks are inserted by clang's C front end, so on LLVM IR input it checks nothing. No C is compiled in slice 0, and the LLVM-level undefined behaviour of the printed IR (poison, `unreachable`) stays P_print's.
branches/D-trusted-base.diff:348: 12. **Stack bound** (owner, 2026-11-07; implemented in the same script and `lib/stack_bound.py`; the `aarch64-macos` run check with LLDB, 2026-11-08): a stack-bound fact for each example, derived from the final artifact (the linked executable, not the IR) for both backends, and checked against a run. The analysis follows every path of the Concrete-owned code, instruction by instruction from an explicit set of modelled instructions, (`main`, `concrete_rt_write_all`, `concrete_body`) over the disassembly's control-flow graph, tracking the stack and frame pointers' depth below `main`'s entry and every byte addressed relative to them (so the red zone), refusing an access deeper than the target's red zone below the stack pointer (128 bytes on x86-64 System V and on Apple arm64, none under AAPCS64 on Linux: deeper bytes may be overwritten by a signal handler), and fails closed on anything it does not model (an instruction outside the set, another stack-pointer write, frame- or link-register writeback, an indirect branch or call, a stack pointer copied out, an indexed stack address, a stack read of bytes the function has not itself written on every path since its last call (so no stale saved frame pointer is ever loaded), a saved slot read other than by the modelled restore, two depths meeting, recursion, a call to a function neither owned nor a PLT/stub). The external calls (`write`, `_exit`, `abort`) are listed with the depth at their entry; their own use, and that `_exit` and `abort` do not return, are the libc premise. On x86-64 Linux the run check single-steps the owned code under the pinned gdb and requires the measured depth to be within the bound, for a complete run only (stopped first at `main`, back at each external call's return address, no signal, the expected exit status). The measured depth is the lowest of the stack pointer and every stack byte a stepped instruction addresses (`lib/stack_access.py` evaluates each memory operand), so a red-zone access is counted, and one deeper than the red zone is an error. A control links a `main` that stores at the red zone's edge without moving the stack pointer and requires bound 128 and measured 128 (on AArch64 it first allocates and frees a 144-byte frame with a pre-indexed store, judged against the new stack pointer, so 144); with a 120-byte red zone the same run gives no measurement. On Mach-O each stub entry's imported symbol comes from the indirect symbol table (`llvm-objdump --macho --indirect-symbols`), since the disassembly names only the `__stubs` section. On `aarch64-macos` the run check is the same, under Apple's LLDB (`/usr/bin/lldb`, `lib/stack_measure_lldb.py`; an external call's return address is the link register); LLDB is not pinned (Apple's signed debugserver is the only one allowed to attach), so its version is printed in the gate log. `stack.tsv` records bound, measurement and externals.
