branches/A-gate-spec-diff.diff:90: 12. **Stack bound** (owner, 2026-11-07; implemented in the same script and `lib/stack_bound.py`; the `aarch64-macos` run check with LLDB, 2026-11-08): a stack-bound fact for each example, derived from the final artifact (the linked executable, not the IR) for both backends, and checked against a run. The analysis follows every path of the Concrete-owned code, instruction by instruction from an explicit set of modelled instructions, (`main`, `concrete_rt_write_all`, `concrete_body`) over the disassembly's control-flow graph, tracking the stack and frame pointers' depth below `main`'s entry and every byte addressed relative to them (so the red zone), refusing an access deeper than the target's red zone below the stack pointer (128 bytes on x86-64 System V and on Apple arm64, none under AAPCS64 on Linux: deeper bytes may be overwritten by a signal handler), and fails closed on anything it does not model (an instruction outside the set, another stack-pointer write, frame- or link-register writeback, an indirect branch or call, a stack pointer copied out, an indexed stack address, a stack read of bytes the function has not itself written on every path since its last call (so no stale saved frame pointer is ever loaded), a saved slot read other than by the modelled restore, two depths meeting, recursion, a call to a function neither owned nor a PLT/stub). The external calls (`write`, `_exit`, `abort`) are listed with the depth at their entry; their own use, and that `_exit` and `abort` do not return, are the libc premise. On x86-64 Linux the run check single-steps the owned code under the pinned gdb and requires the measured depth to be within the bound, for a complete run only (stopped first at `main`, back at each external call's return address, no signal, the expected exit status). The measured depth is the lowest of the stack pointer and every stack byte a stepped instruction addresses (`lib/stack_access.py` evaluates each memory operand), so a red-zone access is counted, and one deeper than the red zone is an error. A control links a `main` that stores at the red zone's edge without moving the stack pointer and requires bound 128 and measured 128 (on AArch64 it first allocates and frees a 144-byte frame with a pre-indexed store, judged against the new stack pointer, so 144); with a 120-byte red zone the same run gives no measurement. On Mach-O each stub entry's imported symbol comes from the indirect symbol table (`llvm-objdump --macho --indirect-symbols`), since the disassembly names only the `__stubs` section. On `aarch64-macos` the run check is the same, under Apple's LLDB (`/usr/bin/lldb`, `lib/stack_measure_lldb.py`; an external call's return address is the link register); LLDB is not pinned (Apple's signed debugserver is the only one allowed to attach), so its version is printed in the gate log. `stack.tsv` records bound, measurement and externals.
